# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` service of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: an HTTP
service in TypeScript on Node, run as a container on Amazon ECS on Fargate
behind an Application Load Balancer, keeping items in a DynamoDB table, with
its infrastructure as a CDK app. Scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## The API

The load balancer serves HTTPS on port 443 with the certificate the deployer
names, and redirects plain HTTP to it.

| Route             | Body                                | Answers                                    |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `PUT /items/{id}` | `{ "name": "Lamp", "quantity": 3 }` | 200 and the item; 400 for a bad id or body |
| `GET /items/{id}` |                                     | 200 and the item; 404 when there is none   |
| `GET /health`     |                                     | 200 while the process runs                 |

An id is 1 to 64 letters, digits, `-` or `_`. A name is 1 to 200 characters;
a quantity a whole number, 0 or more. A body is at most 64 KiB. Every error is
JSON with a `message`. A `PUT` replaces the whole item, so repeating it is
harmless.

## Signing in

Every route but `GET /health` wants an access token of the stack's Cognito
user pool, sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; without one, or with one
of another pool or app client, the answer is 401. The stack outputs
`UserPoolId`, `UserPoolClientId` and `Issuer`, and gives the server the first
two as `USER_POOL_ID` and `USER_POOL_CLIENT_ID`. Nobody signs themselves up:
create a user, set their password, then sign in for a token.

```sh
POOL=<UserPoolId> CLIENT=<UserPoolClientId>
aws cognito-idp admin-create-user --user-pool-id "$POOL" \
  --username ada@example.com --message-action SUPPRESS
aws cognito-idp admin-set-user-password --user-pool-id "$POOL" \
  --username ada@example.com --password '<a password>' --permanent
TOKEN=$(aws cognito-idp initiate-auth --client-id "$CLIENT" \
  --auth-flow USER_PASSWORD_AUTH \
  --auth-parameters 'USERNAME=ada@example.com,PASSWORD=<a password>' \
  --query AuthenticationResult.AccessToken --output text)
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" https://<the certificate's domain>/items/lamp
```

A password is at least 12 characters, with a lower and an upper case letter,
a digit and a symbol. An access token lasts an hour. A user may add an
authenticator app (`associate-software-token`); set the pool's `mfa` to
`Mfa.REQUIRED` in `infra/user_pool.ts` to make every user use one.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci           # install the exact packages in package-lock.json
npm test         # unit tests and the stacks' assertions, with vitest
npm run bundle   # bundle the service into dist/main.cjs with esbuild
npm run synth    # write the development CloudFormation templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

Run it locally with
`TABLE_NAME=<table> USER_POOL_ID=<pool> USER_POOL_CLIENT_ID=<client> node dist/main.cjs`
after `npm run bundle`; it listens on `PORT`, 8080 by default, and reads AWS
credentials and the region the SDK's usual way. It traces only when
`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` names an OpenTelemetry collector.

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth runs offline, and the
stack runs a prebuilt image its `ImageUri` parameter names, so it needs
neither Docker nor credentials.

## Tiers and operations

The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place they run, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production --all`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production.
Production keeps the table when the stack goes, protects it and the stack
from deletion, recovers it to any second of the last 35 days, backs it up
daily with AWS Backup, traces every request through an AWS Distro for
OpenTelemetry collector beside the app into X-Ray, keeps logs a year and has
a NAT gateway in each of its two zones; development throws its data away
with the stack, traces nothing, keeps logs a month and has one NAT gateway.
Both tiers alarm, have a dashboard, and roll back a deployment whose answers
are over 5 % 5xx for three minutes, or whose tasks fail their health check.
The app has the same CPU and memory in both; production adds the
collector's to the task.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the backups' retention and copy
region, the recovery objectives and who the alarms tell, is in
`infra/operations.ts`, which C4 Factory renders from the Container's
`spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it. The alert email is the
stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which a deploy can set per environment. Each
stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the load balancer's DNS name, and
`AlarmTopicArn`, the topic its alarms notify.

The image ships by digest. A release builds the `Dockerfile`'s image and
pushes it to the registry by its digest; a deploy copies that digest into
the CDK bootstrap's ECR repository
(`cdk-<qualifier>-container-assets-<account>-<region>`) and passes the copy,
`<account>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com/<repository>@sha256:<digest>`, as
`ImageUri`. The task pulls only from that repository, so the image a stack
runs is exactly the one its release built.

Deploying is for the stack's owner, with their own credentials:
`cdk bootstrap` once per account and region, the image pushed to the
bootstrap's repository, then
`cdk deploy -c tier=production --all --parameters {{ .Container }}:CertificateArn=<arn> --parameters {{ .Container }}:ImageUri=<image>`
with an ACM certificate for the service's domain, and a DNS record pointing that
domain at the `EntryPoint` output. `--all` deploys the stack that holds the
copy vault too when `copyToRegion` is set, and the parameter names the
service stack, the only one that takes it.

## Layout

- `src/main.ts` is the entry point the image runs: it starts tracing first
  (`src/start_tracing.ts`, `src/tracing.ts`: OpenTelemetry, only when the
  stack names a collector), reads `TABLE_NAME` and `PORT`, creates the
  DynamoDB client once, starts the HTTP server and stops it on `SIGTERM`.
- `src/server.ts` reads each request (at most 64 KiB) and writes the answer;
  `src/api.ts` routes a request, checks its input and answers with a status
  code; `src/items.ts` holds the rules, free of AWS; `src/item_store.ts`
  keeps items in DynamoDB behind the `ItemStore` interface tests fake.
- `Dockerfile` bundles the service with esbuild and runs it on Node as the
  `node` user; `.dockerignore` keeps everything else out of the image.
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stacks for a tier; `infra/service_stack.ts`
  is the main one: the VPC, the table, the cluster, the task (with the
  collector in production) and its service, the load balancer with its
  access logs, the alarms (`infra/alarms.ts`) and, in production, the
  backups (`infra/backups.ts`). The stack is named after the package.
  `infra/operations.ts` holds what the Container promises.
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to restore the data, roll a
  deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
